/**
@mainpage multobjopt

@section overview Overview

multobjopt solves bounded engineering design problems with one or more scalar
objectives, scalar restrictions, and an optional coupled validation callback.
It provides simulated annealing, a Pareto-aware genetic algorithm, Box's
complex method, projected numerical-gradient descent, and structural automatic
selection. The public API is declared in the @ref multobjopt namespace.

@section model Problem model

- Add between 1 and 25 named bounded parameters with
  multobjopt::problem::add_parameter(). A zero resolution is continuous; a
  positive resolution quantizes the parameter.
- Add minimizing or maximizing functions with
  multobjopt::problem::add_objective(). Positive weights define the scalarized
  ordering, while objective senses define Pareto dominance.
- Add restrictions with multobjopt::problem::add_restriction(). A value greater
  than or equal to zero is feasible; a negative value is a violation.
- Optionally use multobjopt::problem::set_validation() for final acceptance
  based on all objective and restriction results.

@section running Running an optimization

Configure multobjopt::optimizer_options and pass the problem to
multobjopt::optimize(). The returned multobjopt::optimization_result contains
the scalarized best design, every feasible nondominated design retained in the
Pareto archive, the actual algorithm used, and run statistics.

A result with no accepted candidate uses
multobjopt::termination_reason::no_feasible_design. The
multobjopt::evaluated_design::valid flag distinguishes a finite but
constraint-infeasible analysis from a non-finite model result. Restriction
scales can normalize violation magnitudes measured in different engineering
units.

@code{.cpp}
multobjopt::optimizer_options options;
options.algorithm = multobjopt::optimization_algorithm::automatic;
options.max_evaluations = 10'000;

const auto result = multobjopt::optimize(problem_definition, options);
for (const auto& design : result.pareto_front) {
    // Inspect design.parameters, design.objectives, and design.restrictions.
}
@endcode

See the repository README and the current programs under `examples/` for a
growing collection of complete buildable examples.

@section headers Public header layout

Applications can include `multobjopt/multobjopt.hpp` for the complete API.
Focused self-contained headers are also available: `types.hpp`, `problem.hpp`,
`options.hpp`, `result.hpp`, `evaluation.hpp`, and `optimize.hpp`. Their file
documentation describes callback lifetimes, units, validation, feasibility,
and result ownership in detail. Generated `version.hpp` exposes the semantic
version and, when available, Git description, commit, branch, and dirty state.
*/
